"Biographies of 張釋之 and 馮唐"

The Commandant of Justice 張釋之 was a man of 堵陽， whose courtesy name was 季. He lived together with his elder brother 仲. Through his wealth he became a Cavalry Attendant, serving Emperor 孝文， but for ten years he received no promotion and was not known. 釋之 said: "Long service in office diminishes my brother's property, and I have not succeeded." He wished to resign his post and return home. The Commandant of the Palace 袁盎 knew of his worth and regretted his departure, so he requested that 釋之 be transferred to fill the post of Messenger-Receiver. When 釋之 had finished attending court, he thereupon spoke of matters of practical advantage. Emperor 文 said: "Lower it; do not speak so loftily—make it something that can be put into practice today." Thereupon 釋之 spoke of the affairs between 秦 and 漢， and of why 秦 failed and 漢 rose, at length. Emperor 文 praised him, and appointed 釋之 as Supervisor of the Messenger-Receivers.

釋之 accompanied the Emperor on an excursion, ascending the tiger enclosure. The Emperor questioned the Warden of 上林 about the registers of the various birds and beasts; after more than ten questions, the Warden looked left and right, unable to answer any of them. The Forester of the tiger enclosure, standing nearby, answered in place of the Warden regarding the registers of birds and beasts that the Emperor asked about, in great detail, wishing thereby to display his ability to respond orally without end. Emperor 文 said: "Should not officials be like this? The Warden is worthless!" He then decreed that 釋之 appoint the Forester as Prefect of 上林. After a long while, 釋之 stepped forward and said: "Your Majesty, what manner of man do you regard 絳侯 周勃 as?" The Emperor said: "A man of long standing and virtue." He asked again: "And what manner of man is 東陽侯 張相如？" The Emperor again said: "A man of long standing and virtue." 釋之 said: "That 絳侯 and 東陽侯 are called men of long standing and virtue—yet these two men, when speaking of affairs, could scarcely get the words out of their mouths. Would they emulate this Forester's chattering, sharp tongue and quick responses! Moreover, 秦 employed clerks of pen and knife; the officials vied with one another in hastiness, severity, and minute scrutiny, yet their defect was mere formalism, without the substance of compassion. For this reason, their faults went unheard, and by decline it reached the point of the Second Emperor, when the realm crumbled like earth. Now Your Majesty, because of the Forester's eloquence of tongue, promotes him by leaps; your servant fears that the realm will follow this trend, vying in verbal cleverness without substance. Moreover, those below are influenced by those above more swiftly than shadow follows form or echo follows sound; your actions cannot but be examined carefully." Emperor 文 said: "Good." He then desisted and did not appoint the Forester.

The Emperor approached his carriage and summoned 釋之 to ride beside him. Proceeding slowly, he questioned 釋之 about the failings of 秦. 釋之 answered fully with plain speech. Upon reaching the palace, the Emperor appointed 釋之 as Prefect of the Public Carriages.

Some time later, the Heir Apparent and the Prince of 梁 rode together in a carriage to court, and did not alight at the 司馬 Gate. Thereupon 釋之 pursued and stopped the Heir Apparent and the Prince of 梁, not permitting them to enter the palace gate. He then impeached them for not alighting at the public gate, showing disrespect, and reported it. When 薄太后 heard of this, Emperor 文 removed his cap and apologized, saying: "I have not been careful in teaching my sons." 薄太后 then sent an envoy bearing a decree pardoning the Heir Apparent and the Prince of 梁, after which they were allowed to enter. Emperor 文 thereby considered 釋之 extraordinary and appointed him Grand Master of the Palace.

Some time later, he became Commandant of the Palace. He accompanied the Emperor on an excursion to 霸陵， where they sat on the north side overlooking the precipice. At this time 慎夫人 was in attendance. The Emperor pointed out to 慎夫人 the road to 新豐， saying: "This is the road that runs to 邯鄲." He had 慎夫人 play the se, and the Emperor himself sang to the zither. His mood was sorrowful and melancholy. He turned and said to his ministers: "Alas! If one used the stone of the northern mountain for the outer coffin, and used ramie floss, cut and spread about, and lacquered the spaces between with lacquer, could it ever be moved?" All those around said: "Good." 釋之 stepped forward and said: "If there is something desirable within it, then even if you seal it within the Southern Mountain, there will still be cracks; if there is nothing desirable within it, then even without a stone outer coffin, what cause for sorrow would there be!" Emperor 文 praised him. Thereafter he appointed 釋之 as Commandant of Justice.

Some time later, the Emperor traveled out over the 中渭 Bridge. A man ran out from under the bridge, and the horses of the imperial carriage were startled. Thereupon he sent cavalry to arrest him and handed him over to the Commandant of Justice. 釋之 interrogated him. The man said: "I came from the county seat. Hearing the cry to clear the road, I hid under the bridge. After a long while, thinking the procession had already passed, I came out, saw the imperial carriage and cavalry, and simply ran." The Commandant of Justice submitted his judgment: one man had violated the road-clearing; the sentence should be a fine. Emperor 文 was angry and said: "This man personally startled my horses. My horses were fortunately gentle; had it been other horses, would they not surely have injured me? Yet the Commandant of Justice sentences him only to a fine!" 釋之 said: "The law is what the Son of Heaven shares in common with all under heaven. Now if the law is thus and you make it heavier, then the law will not be trusted by the people. Moreover, at that moment, had Your Majesty ordered him executed on the spot, that would have been the end of it. Now that the case has been referred to the Commandant of Justice—the Commandant of Justice is the balance of all under heaven. If it tips once, then the application of the law throughout the realm will be either light or heavy; where shall the people place their hands and feet? May Your Majesty examine this." After a long while, the Emperor said: "The Commandant of Justice's judgment is correct."

Later, someone stole a jade ring from before the seat in the temple of 高. He was caught. Emperor 文 was angry and referred him to the Commandant of Justice for trial. 釋之, in accordance with the statutes—those who steal the garments or utensils of the ancestral temple—submitted a report, sentencing him to exposure in the marketplace. The Emperor was greatly angered and said: "This man is without the Way—he stole the vessels of the temple of the former Emperor. The reason I referred him to the Commandant of Justice was that I wished his whole clan exterminated, yet you report according to the law. This is not my intention in reverently maintaining the ancestral temple." 釋之 removed his cap, knocked his head to the ground, and apologized, saying: "The law being thus is sufficient. Moreover, when crimes are equal, yet they are differentiated according to whether they are rebellious or compliant. Now if one steals the vessels of the ancestral temple and his clan is exterminated—if by some chance, one in ten thousand, a foolish man were to take a single handful of earth from 長陵， how would Your Majesty increase the punishment for that?" After a long time, Emperor 文 spoke of it with the Empress Dowager, and then approved the Commandant of Justice's judgment. At this time, the Commandant of the Capital 條侯 周亞夫 and the Chancellor of 梁， 山都侯 王恬開， seeing that 釋之 held his opinions with fairness, formed close friendships with him. Commandant of Justice 張 was from this time praised throughout the realm.

Later, Emperor 文 died, and Emperor 景 was established. 釋之 was afraid. He pleaded illness. He wished to resign and depart, fearing that a great punishment would arrive; he wished to appear and apologize, but did not know how it would go. Following the plan of 王生， he finally appeared and apologized, and Emperor 景 did not pursue the matter.

As for 王生， he was skilled in the words of 黃老 and was a retired scholar. He was once summoned to reside at court. The Three Excellencies and Nine Ministers all stood assembled. 王生, being an old man, said: "My stocking has come loose," and turned to Commandant of Justice 張， saying: "Tie my stocking for me!" 釋之 knelt and tied it. When this was done, someone said to 王生： "Why did you alone humiliate Commandant of Justice 張 before the court, making him kneel and tie your stocking?" 王生 said: "I am old and lowly, and I reckon that in the end I can be of no benefit to Commandant of Justice 張. Commandant of Justice 張 is at present a famous minister of the realm. I therefore deliberately humiliated the Commandant of Justice, making him kneel and tie my stocking, because I wished thereby to add weight to him." When the various lords heard of this, they considered 王生 worthy and esteemed Commandant of Justice 張 all the more.

Commandant of Justice 張 served Emperor 景 for a year and more, then became Chancellor of the Prince of 淮南—this was still on account of his former offense. After a long time, 釋之 died. His son was named 張摯， courtesy name 長公， whose office reached that of Grand Master; he was dismissed. Because he could not accommodate himself to his age, he never held office for the rest of his life.

As for 馮唐， his grandfather was a man of 趙. His father moved to 代. When 漢 rose, they moved to 安陵. 唐 was noted for filial piety and became Chief of the Palace Attendants, serving Emperor 文. Emperor 文, passing in his carriage, asked 唐： "Old father, how did you come to be an Attendant? Where is your home?" 唐 answered fully with the truth. Emperor 文 said: "When I resided in 代， my Provisioner Supervisor 高袪 often spoke to me of the worthiness of the 趙 general 李齊， who fought below 鉅鹿. Now every time I eat, my thoughts have never been absent from 鉅鹿. Do you, old father, know of this?" 唐 replied: "He was still not equal to 廉頗 and 李牧 as generals." The Emperor said: "How so?" 唐 said: "When my grandfather was in 趙， he was a commander of officers and men, and was a friend of 李牧. My father was formerly Chancellor of 代 and was a friend of the 趙 general 李齊， and knew his character." When the Emperor had heard of the character of 廉頗 and 李牧， he was greatly pleased, and slapping his thigh, said: "Alas! If only I could have 廉頗 and 李牧 as my generals, how could I worry about the 匈奴！" 唐 said: "Your servant humbly submits: even if Your Majesty could obtain 廉頗 and 李牧， you could not employ them." The Emperor was angry, rose, and entered the forbidden chambers. After a long while, he summoned 唐 and rebuked him, saying: "Why did you disgrace me before everyone? Was there no private place?" 唐 apologized, saying: "Your humble servant is an uncouth man who knows not what is taboo."

At this time, the 匈奴 had newly made a great incursion into 朝那， killing the Commandant of 北地， 卬. The Emperor was concerned about the 胡 invaders, and so finally asked 唐 again: "How do you know that I could not employ 廉頗 and 李牧？" 唐 replied: "Your servant has heard that when the kings of high antiquity dispatched generals, they knelt and pushed the wheel of the chariot, saying: 'Within the threshold, I control it; beyond the threshold, the general controls it.' Military merit, ranks, and rewards were all decided abroad, and reported upon return. This is no empty saying. Your servant's grandfather said that when 李牧 was a general of 趙 guarding the frontier, the taxes of the military markets he used entirely himself to feast his soldiers; rewards and gifts were decided abroad, not interfered with from within. He entrusted him with responsibility and demanded success, and so 李牧 was able to exhaust his wisdom and ability: he dispatched a thousand three hundred selected chariots, thirteen thousand crossbow-armed cavalry, and a hundred thousand men worth a hundred in gold. Thereby he drove north the Chanyu, defeated the 東胡， destroyed the 澹林， checked powerful 秦 in the west, and supported 韓 and 魏 in the south. At that time, 趙 nearly became hegemon. Later it happened that King 遷 of 趙 was established, whose mother was a singing girl. When King 遷 was established, he employed the slander of 郭開， and finally executed 李牧， ordering 顏聚 to replace him. Thereby the army was broken and the soldiers fled north, and they were captured and destroyed by 秦. Now your servant has privately heard that 魏尚 is Governor of 雲中， and that the taxes of his military markets he uses entirely to feast his officers and men; he brings out his private maintenance money, and once every five days slaughters an ox, feasting his guests, military officers, and retainers. Thereby the 匈奴 keep far away and do not approach the fortifications of 雲中. The invaders once entered, and 尚 led chariots and cavalry to attack them, killing many of them. Now these soldiers are all sons of common families, who rose from the fields to join the army—how would they know the registers and tallies of the five-man squads? All day long they fight with their strength, taking heads and capturing prisoners, and report their merits to the headquarters. If a single word does not correspond, the clerks apply the law to them. Their rewards are not carried out, yet the clerks in upholding the law are always heeded. Your servant is foolish, but considers that Your Majesty's laws are too exacting, your rewards too light, your punishments too heavy. Moreover, the Governor of 雲中， 魏尚， was convicted because in reporting merit the heads and captives were short by six; Your Majesty referred him to the clerks, stripped his rank, and sentenced him to penal labor. Speaking from this, even if Your Majesty could obtain 廉頗 and 李牧， you could not employ them. Your servant is truly foolish and has touched upon taboo matters. Death! Death!" Emperor 文 was pleased. That day he ordered 馮唐 to bear the tally and pardon 魏尚， restoring him as Governor of 雲中， and appointed 唐 as Commandant of Chariots and Cavalry, in charge of the chariot soldiers of the Commandant of the Capital and of the commanderies and kingdoms.

In the seventh year, Emperor 景 was established, and 唐 was made Chancellor of 楚； he was dismissed. When Emperor 武 was established, he sought out the worthy and good, and 馮唐 was recommended. 唐 was then over ninety years old and could no longer serve as an official, so 唐's son 馮遂 was made an Attendant. 遂， whose courtesy name was 王孫， was also an extraordinary man, and was a friend of mine.

The Grand Historian says: The words of 張季 concerning men of long standing and virtue—upholding the law without bending to others' wishes; the discourse of Lord 馮 on generals and commanders—how full of flavor! How full of flavor! The saying goes: "If you do not know a man, look at his friends." What these two lords praised and recited could be inscribed in the halls of the temple. The Documents say: "Without partiality, without partisanship, the kingly way is broad and expansive; without partisanship, without partiality, the kingly way is level and smooth." 張季 and Lord 馮 came close to this. [end of text]


